  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Duty Cycle and DC Value",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the DC Fourier-series coefficient for a rectangular periodic signal by converting duty cycle and signal levels into the average value over one period.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Duty Cycle and DC Value",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Duty Cycle and DC Value",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a rectangular periodic signal, the duty cycle $D$ is the fraction of each period that the signal spends at the high level. If the signal is high for a time $\\tau$ inside a period $T$, then $D = \\frac{\\tau}{T}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D = \\frac{\\tau}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The DC Fourier-series coefficient is the average value over one period. For a periodic signal $x(t)$, compute that average by integrating over one full period and dividing by $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = \\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T x(t)\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the simplest pulse train, $x(t)=A$ on the on-time of length $\\tau$ and $x(t)=0$ elsewhere. Only the high interval contributes to the integral, so the average becomes the high level times the duty cycle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = A D",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule to carry forward is simple: read the duty cycle from the on-time, then take the one-period average to get the DC value. Later rectangular signals will use the same average-value idea, even when the signal levels change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Duty Cycle and DC Value",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing DC Value from a Given Duty Cycle",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic rectangular signal is $12$ for $\\frac{1}{3}$ of each period and $0$ for the rest. What is its DC value?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the duty cycle is already given: $D=\\frac{1}{3}$. The signal is high for that fraction of each period and $0$ for the rest, so the DC value is the one-period average.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = 12\\cdot\\frac{1}{3} + 0\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The high level contributes $12$ for the first third of the period, and the low level contributes $0$ for the remaining two thirds. So the average becomes the high level times the duty cycle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the DC value is $4$. In Fourier-series language, that average is the DC coefficient for this rectangular signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Duty Cycle and DC Value",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Duty Cycle from Pulse Width and Period",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A rectangular signal is high for $2$ ms of every $5$ ms period and is $0$ otherwise. If the high level is $10$, what is its DC value?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the pulse width and period are already given: the signal is high for $2$ ms out of every $5$ ms period. That means the first step is to convert the geometry into duty cycle $D = \\tau/T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D = \\frac{\\tau}{T} = \\frac{2}{5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use the average-value formula for the rectangular signal. With high level $10$ and low level $0$, the DC value is the high level weighted by the high-time fraction and the low level weighted by the remaining fraction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = 10\\cdot\\frac{2}{5} + 0\\cdot\\frac{3}{5}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Simplifying gives $\\bar{x}=4$. So the DC value is $4$, and the key move is converting pulse width and period into duty cycle before averaging.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Duty Cycle and DC Value",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing DC Value for a Two-Level Rectangular Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic rectangular signal is $6$ for $\\frac{1}{4}$ of each period and $-1$ for the rest. What is its DC value?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a two-level rectangular signal. The high level is $6$ for a fraction $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the period, so the low level $-1$ fills the remaining $\\frac{3}{4}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + (-1) \\cdot \\frac{3}{4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify each contribution. The high level contributes $\\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}$, and the low level contributes $-\\frac{3}{4}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the DC value is $\\frac{3}{4}$. The key move is the weighted average of the two levels, with each level multiplied by the fraction of time it appears.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Duty Cycle and DC Value",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Zero DC Value from Balanced Signal Levels",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic rectangular signal is $4$ for $\\frac{1}{3}$ of each period and $-2$ for the rest. What is its DC value?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the signal has two levels and the weighted contributions are meant to cancel. The high level is $4$ for $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the period, so the low level $-2$ fills the remaining $\\frac{2}{3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + (-2) \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compare the two weighted terms. The positive contribution is $\\frac{4}{3}$, and the negative contribution is $-\\frac{4}{3}$, so they have equal magnitude and opposite sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\bar{x} = \\frac{4}{3} - \\frac{4}{3} = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the DC value is $0$. The key recognition is that balanced weighted levels cancel exactly, so the average over one period is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
